MODIFICATIONS
-------------


        A. Slip Correction for Small Particles
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
The Cunningham slip correction factor has been added to:
- Brownian model (Stokes–Einstein)
- Inertial models (fullStokes, Manninen)
- sectionalSubGridDepositionVelocityFvPatchVectorField.C

Reference: Eq. 3.20 in Hinds (Aerosol Technology, 2nd edition).


-----------------------------------------------------------------------

        B. Size-Dependent Dynamic Shape Factor
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Size-dependent shape factor corrections are now included in:
- Brownian model (Stokes–Einstein)
- Inertial models (fullStokes, snDrift, Manninen)
- sectionalSubGridDepositionVelocityFvPatchVectorField.C
- Coagulation model

Input parameters for shape factor:
- Fractal dimension (frdim) [for spherical shape, frDim = 3]
- Monomer radius (monorad)
- Monomer density (monorho)
- Optional: Pre-factor and exponent terms (dependent on frdim and flow regime).

****These parameters are defined under 'sfparam': define 
				Fractal dimension (frdim), 
				Monomer radius (monorad) and 
				Monomer density (monorho)

Supported particle shapes (particleShape):

	- solid: sets automatically frdim=3, pre-factors=1, exponents=0. No need to define monomer radius, monomer densiity or pre factor and exponents explicitly
	
	- fractal: requires frdim, monorad, monorho; Supported default frdim values are 2.49, 2.25, 2.20, 1.8. No need to define pre factor and exponents explicitly, it will be auto-assigned.
  		[Ref. Jayant et al., Nuclear Engineering and Design, 2025, Vol. 440]
  		
	- custom: fully user-defined frdim, monorad, monorho, pre-factors (dysfPfFm, dysfPfTr, dysfPfCont) and 
	                             exponent terms (dysfExpFm, dysfExpTr, dysfExpCont).
  	          Useful for constant shape factor cases.

For e.g. if a constant shape factor = 1.5 to be used, in that case, set frdim =3, 
                                              pre-factor terms (dysfPfFm, dysfPfTr, dysfPfCont) = 1.5 and 
                                              exponent terms (dysfExpFm, dysfExpTr, dysfExpCont) =0.

Example usage: see Ex. 1.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

          C. Condensation Model
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

New switches introduced:

- Kelvin effect:     KelvinEffect: true/false
- Fuchs–Sutugin:     FuchsCorrection: true/false
- Droplet temperature correction: DropletTemperatureCorrection: true/false

Defaults: all switches are set to false if not specified.

Additional options:

 - SR: Saturation ratio of the surrounding gas (SR = pv/psat), used only when
       DropletTemperatureCorrection is enabled. Typical values are:
    SR = 0.0 → dry air (0% RH)
    SR = 0.55 → 55% RH
    SR = 1.0 → saturated air (100% RH)
    SR > 1.0 → supersaturated conditions

Note: If DropletTemperatureCorrection is enabled, SR must be specified.

 - solute & soluteLimit:
 
    If a solute species is present in the dispersed phase, the solute name
    and a critical solute mass fraction can be specified. When the solute
    mass fraction exceeds the specified limit, evaporation/condensation is
    terminated.


Example usage: see Ex. 2.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

       D. Coagulation Model
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

The fixedSectional.C model now includes coagulation kernels for:
	- Laminar flow: Fuchs’ Brownian coagulation
	- Turbulent flow: Turbulent inertia and turbulent shear models

Turbulent models are compatible with k–ε, k–ω, and k–ω SST models, 
in absence of these models, a fixed ε value can be set as default in fixedSectional.C routine.

The kernel works for both fractal and solid spherical particles. The monomer
radius must be smaller than the smallest size in the input distribution.


[Ref. Jacobson, Fundamentals of Atmospheric Modeling, 2004]

